@startuml flow-auth
' Sign-in, recovery and Google — including the decisions that exist because the
' obvious implementation was wrong. See AUTH_IMPLEMENTATION_PROMPT.md.
'
' Render:  plantuml docs/diagrams/flow-auth.puml

title Flow — signing in, recovering a password, and Google

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #4A5568
  LifeLineBorderColor #7C86A0
  ParticipantBorderColor #7C86A0
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #FDFDFD
}

actor User
participant "Angular SPA" as SPA
participant "AuthService" as API
database "PostgreSQL" as DB
participant "Brevo\n(HTTP API)" as Mail
participant "Google" as Google

== Password sign-in ==

User -> SPA : identifier + password
SPA -> API : POST /api/auth/login
activate API
API -> DB : findByUsername(identifier)
alt not found and identifier contains "@"
  API -> DB : findByEmail(normalised)
  note right
    Recovery mails a code to an **email** and
    finds the account by it; sign-in finds
    accounts by **username**. For anyone who
    arrived through Google those differ — the
    username is generated from their display
    name — so without this fallback a user can
    recover an account they cannot then sign
    in to, and is told "invalid username or
    password" for a password that is correct.
  end note
end
API -> API : skip accounts with **no password hash**\n(a Google account has none;\nthe encoder throws on null)
API -> API : BCrypt.matches

alt MFA enrolled
  API --> SPA : MFA_REQUIRED + challenge token (typ=mfa)
  note right of API
    The challenge token carries **no role** and is
    rejected by JwtAuthFilter, so it can never act
    as a session on its own.
  end note
  User -> SPA : PIN / TOTP / passkey
  SPA -> API : POST /api/auth/verify-mfa
  API --> SPA : full access token
else no second factor
  API --> SPA : full access token
end
deactivate API

== Forgotten password ==

User -> SPA : "Forgot your password?"
SPA -> API : POST /api/auth/otp/request {email}
activate API
API -> DB : findByEmail
API -> API : generate code, store hashed, 10-minute TTL

alt demo mode ON
  API --> SPA : the code, shown on screen
else demo mode OFF
  API -> Mail : POST /v3/smtp/email
  alt 201 Created
    Mail --> API : sent
    API --> SPA : "check your email" — **no code in the response**
  else delivery failed
    Mail --> API : error
    API -> API : delete the stored code
    API --> SPA : **503**
    note right of API
      The bug this replaced: the code was hidden
      only when delivery SUCCEEDED, so with no
      provider configured it fell through and was
      returned anyway — handing a working
      credential to anyone able to trigger a
      send failure.
    end note
  end
end
deactivate API

User -> SPA : enters the code
SPA -> API : POST /api/auth/otp/verify
API --> SPA : token carrying **vbc** (verified by code)

SPA -> User : "Set a password before continuing"
note over SPA
  Without this step, somebody who forgot their
  password signs in by code forever and never
  recovers the account — the one-time code
  becomes a permanent workaround.
end note

User -> SPA : new password
SPA -> API : POST /api/auth/change-password {newPassword}
activate API
API -> API : vbc present **and** issued < 15 min ago?
note right
  Bounded, and dropped on refresh. A tab left
  open on a shared machine must not still be
  able to rewrite the password tomorrow.
end note
API -> DB : save new BCrypt hash
API --> SPA : {changed, **username**}
deactivate API
SPA --> User : "Sign in with the username X"

== Google ==

User -> SPA : "Continue with Google"
SPA -> Google : OAuth2 authorisation
Google --> API : callback with verified identity
activate API
API -> DB : findByEmail

alt account exists
  API --> SPA : token **with the role it already has**
  note right of API
    Google proves who somebody is. It does not
    decide what they may do — the role is never
    changed here.
  end note
else unknown identity
  alt OAUTH_AUTO_PROVISION = false (default)
    API --> SPA : redirect to /login?error=…
    note right of API
      Refused, and redirected back to the FRONTEND.
      Letting it throw renders the backend's error
      page on the backend's domain, which looks
      like a crash and strands the user off-site.
    end note
  else auto-provision enabled
    API -> DB : create with OAUTH_DEFAULT_ROLE (SHAREHOLDER)
    API --> SPA : token
  end
end
deactivate API

@enduml
